{"duration_api_ms":119175,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"a18b6387-8e6c-4f29-8e5e-a14c5c7225c9","total_cost_usd":0.3368868,"usage":{"input_tokens":14,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15013,"cache_read_input_tokens":152429,"output_tokens":11564,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":7920},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15013},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":14,"outputTokens":11564,"cacheReadInputTokens":152429,"cacheCreationInputTokens":15013,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3368868,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":7920,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":7,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"The answer is in `/work/answer.md` and I've marked it FINAL on the board. Sol and I worked out the same condition separately. Sol wrote the proof, I checked it, and our separate numerical checks found no mismatches.\n\n**Condition.** Assume all values are positive. The Vickrey-nearest point equals the centroid of F exactly when one of these holds:\n\n- **P's value is small:** v_P ≤ min(v_A, v_B, v_C). Then F is the whole triangle {p ≥ 0, Σp = v_P}.\n- **P's value is large:** v_P ≥ the sum of the two largest single-item values. Then all three VCG prices are positive and F is again a symmetric triangle.\n- **Equal values:** v_A = v_B = v_C.\n\nIf any winner's value is zero, the two points always coincide.\n\n**Proof outline:**\n1. **Simplifying the core.** On the plane Σp = v_P the pair constraints are the same as p_k ≤ v_k. So R = v_P, and F = {VCG ≤ p ≤ v, Σp = v_P}: a box cut by a plane.\n2. **Shifting by the VCG point.** Move the origin to the VCG point. Write Δ = Σv − v_P, cap_i = min(v_i, Δ) and s = Σcap − Δ. F becomes {0 ≤ y_i ≤ cap_i, Σy = s}.\n3. **The nearest point.** It is y_i = min(cap_i, t), with the same t for every coordinate. The centroid lies strictly inside every cap. So if the two points coincide, no cap binds and the point is (s/3, s/3, s/3).\n4. **The centroid.** Swapping two coordinates shows that if cap_i < cap_j and cap_i < s, the centroid's i-coordinate is strictly smaller than its j-coordinate. So the centroid has equal coordinates exactly when all caps are equal or every cap is at least s. Translating back to the values gives the three cases above.\n\n**Example where they differ:** (v_P, v_A, v_B, v_C) = (2, 1, 2, 2). VCG prices are all zero and F is a trapezoid. The Vickrey-nearest point is (2/3, 2/3, 2/3), and no value cap binds there. The centroid is (4/9, 7/9, 7/9).\n\n**Checks:** My check built F directly from all seven core constraints in exact arithmetic and confirmed R = v_P. Over 20,000 random instances it found no mismatches with the condition. Sol's check of all 2,052 small-integer cases (values 1 to 6) also found none.","ttft_ms":2037,"type":"result","duration_ms":272520,"uuid":"2c84ed92-f9cc-46c3-abe3-3bf27734ab80","ttft_stream_ms":1669,"time_to_request_ms":71,"first_content_frame_ms":1669,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
